window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-f391eb60ff6f0e0b"]={"key":"ny_main-f391eb60ff6f0e0b","group":"ny_main","uid":"f391eb60ff6f0e0b","rank":89,"title":"gdpval authored doc real estate broker compliance 001","occupation":"real_estate_brokers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You're Antoni Underwood, the enterprise real estate broker in Marchbank Components’ shared-property team, and Oona Hollingsworth needs you to prepare the client-facing compliance assessment for affiliate Ironvale Freight before its 22 March 2029 decision between renewing its two existing offices and consolidating into one headquarters. The assessment is due to Oona by 5:00 p.m. on 14 March so there is time to correct the decision pack. Jelena Ingram’s client emails describe terms and availability that conflict with the frozen 28 February market snapshot, and Kasper Garrido has already told Ironvale Freight that consolidation will be presented as the lowest-cost option. That statement is now circulating, but nobody has demonstrated that the alternatives were compared on equivalent economic terms. Marchbank is also midway through changing its comparison practice. For packs issued through 31 March, teams may still display the legacy headline-rent-per-square-foot view; from 1 April, the normalized occupancy-cost view becomes the required standard. Ironvale Freight will receive the pack during the transition but may continue using it after the end-state standard takes effect, so the assessment has to work in both periods without implying that the legacy measure controls the decision. The phrase “occupancy cost” is another problem: property colleagues often mean recurring lease and building charges, while finance colleagues use it for all cash required to take and occupy the premises, including one-time fit-out and transition costs. Public committee members routinely read the phrase both ways, so pin down what it means before relying on it. Please produce a concise, public-facing assessment that covers (1) the present compliance and evidence status of the proposed comparison, including whether the circulating lowest-cost description is supportable; (2) what a like-for-like comparison of the two-office renewal alternative and the single-headquarters alternative must account for; (3) how the legacy and end-state presentation practices can coexist during the changeover without confusing their evidentiary weight; (4) neutral decision conditions showing what would have to be true for either alternative to be economically or operationally preferable; (5) prioritized corrective actions that can realistically be completed before 22 March, with clear ownership and expected outputs; and (6) the limitations, evidence labels and commercial disclosures a non-specialist reader needs in order not to mistake an estimate, asking term or provisional availability statement for a verified commitment. This is an assessment of the communication and comparison, not authority to choose for Ironvale Freight. Do not select, score, rank or endorse either alternative, and do not preserve the earlier consolidation claim merely to make the pack consistent with what was said. There are no attachments for this handoff: the snapshot, emails, lease abstracts and financial model are not being supplied for this initial assessment. Do not create market, lease, area or cost figures. Where a fact cannot be verified, make its status and decision consequence clear rather than filling the gap. Minimize references to individual correspondence and include no personal contact details or unnecessary quotations. Write for a broad non-specialist audience, use plain language, and keep the complete PDF to no more than six pages, including any cover, notes or appendix. Write the finished file into the top level of the working directory using exactly the filename `ironvale_lease_comparison_compliance_assessment.pdf`.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `ironvale_lease_comparison_compliance_assessment.pdf` — Client-facing compliance and evidence-readiness assessment for Ironvale Freight’s office lease alternatives. (Place the file at the top level of the working directory.; Use the exact required filename.; Submit one openable PDF containing no more than six pages, including any cover, notes or appendix.; Write for a broad non-specialist audience.; Do not invent market, lease, area or cost figures.; Present facts and decision conditions without selecting, ranking or endorsing an alternative.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/ironvale_lease_comparison_compliance_assessment.pdf","bytes":24061,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/60408311a4a0c35a0a0e3d153f78f39893a95fd388f536219bcc1ccda21cb8bc.pdf","sha256":"60408311a4a0c35a0a0e3d153f78f39893a95fd388f536219bcc1ccda21cb8bc","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"MARCHBANK COMPONENTS / SHARED-PROPERTY TEAM                                                                                               COMPARISON READINESS\n\n\n\n\nCLIENT-FACING ASSESSMENT / 14 MARCH 2029\n\n\nIRONVALE FREIGHT\nLease comparison compliance assessment\nDecision-pack release readiness for the 22 March 2029 office decision\n PREPARED FOR                    Oona Hollingsworth                                   PREPARED BY                 Antoni Underwood\n TEAM                            Marchbank Components shared-property                 ROLE                        Enterprise real estate broker\n                                 team\n\n\nPURPOSE AND SCOPE\n\nThis assessment reviews whether the client-facing comparison is supported by dated evidence, uses a like-for-like economic scope and\ncommunicates uncertainty clearly. It assesses evidence, comparison readiness and client communication. It does not choose an option;\nIronvale Freight retains the decision.\n\n\n  DEFINED TERM: OCCUPANCY COST\n\n  In this assessment, recurring occupancy cost means the charges required to keep and occupy a premises over the lease period: fixed or\n  base rent; applicable operating-expense recoveries or pass-throughs, such as common-area charges, property taxes or insurance; and\n  separately paid utilities and occupancy services. Concessions and landlord contributions are shown as offsets only in the periods and under\n  the conditions where they are verified to apply.\n\n  All-in decision cost keeps that recurring subtotal visible and adds one-time costs needed to take and occupy the premises, including\n  tenant-funded fit-out, transaction or transition costs, move effects, overlap and existing-premises exit or continuing-liability effects. The two\n  layers must remain visibly separate. No cost figure is supplied or asserted in this assessment.\n\n\n\n   CURRENT FINDING\n\n   NOT DECISION-READY FOR A LOWEST-COST DESCRIPTION\n   The material available for this handoff does not substantiate consolidation as the lowest-cost option. The normalized occupancy-cost comparison is not\n   yet decision-ready. The earlier consolidation statement is prior communication requiring correction, not a verified fact, comparison input or presentation\n   label.\n\n\n\nDECISION FRAME\n\n THE CLIENT DECISION                    ALTERNATIVE CASES\n\n 22 March 2029                          Renew both existing offices; or consolidate into one headquarters.\n\n Decision authority                     Ironvale Freight decides. The broker provides a neutral evidence and communication assessment.\n\n Case boundary                          Renewal retains the combined economics of both existing offices. Consolidation includes the new headquarters and the\n                                        economic consequences of exiting or carrying the existing offices.\n\n\nCONTROL DATES\n\n Date                           What it controls                                                Status in this handoff\n\n 28 February 2029               Frozen market-evidence cutoff and required baseline             Snapshot not supplied or reviewed; no market fact is asserted\n\n 14 March 2029, 5:00 p.m.       Assessment due to Oona, leaving time to correct the pack        Current assessment date\n\n 22 March 2029                  Ironvale Freight decision date                                  Later than the evidence cutoff\n\n Through 31 March 2029          Legacy headline-rent-per-square-foot view may still appear      Context and continuity only\n\n From 1 April 2029              Normalized occupancy-cost view is required                      Controlling economic view after cutover\n\n\n\n\nClient-facing working assessment | No commercial commitment                                                                               14 March 2029 | Page 1 of 6\n\fMARCHBANK COMPONENTS / SHARED-PROPERTY TEAM                                                                                                             COMPARISON READINESS\n\n\n\n\n01 / EVIDENCE STATUS\n\nWhat can be said now\nThe handoff identifies a material source conflict and a circulating conclusion, but supplies none of the underlying\nsnapshot, correspondence, lease abstracts or financial model. Status labels below prevent a reader from mistaking a\nstatement for a commitment.\n\nEVIDENCE LABELS USED THROUGHOUT\n\n Label                               Plain-language meaning                                                           How it may be used\n\n Verified fact                       Supported by a reviewed source whose source and effective or as-of date          May support a comparison input, subject to scope checks.\n                                     are recorded.\n\n Third-party statement / quote       A term, availability statement or quotation reported by another party; it is     Use only with source, date and commercial-status\n                                     not automatically a commitment.                                                  disclosure.\n\n Estimate                            A calculated or market-based approximation with a stated method, date            Use for planning or sensitivity, not as a settled term.\n                                     and, where material, a range.\n\n Assumption                          An explicit placeholder selected because an input is missing or uncertain.       Keep visible and test its effect; never disguise it as fact.\n\n Unresolved item                     Missing, conflicting or unconfirmed information.                                 Keep pending and link it to the claim or calculation it blocks;\n                                                                                                                      do not treat it as zero.\n\n\n\nCURRENT SOURCE AND CLAIM STATUS\n\n Material                                           Status for this assessment                                             Decision consequence\n\n Frozen 28 February market snapshot                 Required market baseline, but not supplied or reviewed here.           No rent, area, term or availability from the snapshot can\n                                                                                                                           be stated as verified.\n\n Client-email descriptions of terms and             The handoff identifies a conflict with the frozen snapshot. The        Neither version controls until each is dated, sourced\n availability                                       disputed details and correspondence are not supplied.                  and reconciled.\n\n Two existing-office lease abstracts and            Not supplied.                                                          The renewal and consolidation cases cannot yet be\n headquarters proposal                                                                                                     shown with complete term, area, allocation or exit\n                                                                                                                           scope.\n\n Financial model                                    Not supplied.                                                          Whole-period, annualized, per-area and timing-adjusted\n                                                                                                                           results cannot be validated.\n\n Earlier consolidation-is-lowest-cost statement     Prior communication only; not evidence or a model input.               Withdraw or qualify the label pending a normalized\n                                                                                                                           comparison. Repetition does not substantiate it.\n\n\n\nGAP TO CONSEQUENCE\n\n Unresolved gap                                       What the reader cannot validate\n\n Lease and proposal economics                         Whether recurring charges, recoveries, escalations, concessions, fit-out obligations and existing-premises liabilities are\n                                                      complete for both cases.\n\n Market and availability conflict                     Whether a described term or site condition was available or effective at the relevant date; later information cannot silently\n                                                      replace the baseline.\n\n Common financial basis                               Whether totals use the same horizon, base date, currency, tax treatment, nominal/real basis and treatment of cash timing.\n\n Operational inputs                                   Whether each case has sufficient capacity and functional fit, and whether possession, overlap, move timing or disruption\n                                                      changes the practical result.\n\n\nEvidence rule: every material input must carry an identifiable source and effective or as-of date. Information received after the frozen cutoff is separately dated,\nlabeled and reconciled in a change record.\n\n\n\n\nClient-facing working assessment | No commercial commitment                                                                                              14 March 2029 | Page 2 of 6\n\fMARCHBANK COMPONENTS / SHARED-PROPERTY TEAM                                                                                                     COMPARISON READINESS\n\n\n\n\n02 / LIKE-FOR-LIKE STANDARD\n\nWhat the comparison must include\nA defensible comparison uses the same component scope, time basis and units for both cases. Lease labels and\nheadline rates describe allocation; they do not, by themselves, establish economic comparability.\n\n  RECURRING OCCUPANCY COST                                                             ONE-TIME AND TRANSITION COST\n\n  Show fixed or base rent; applicable recoveries or pass-throughs;                     Show tenant-funded fit-out and transaction or transition costs needed to\n  separately paid utilities and occupancy services; contractual escalations            occupy the premises, including move, furniture, equipment, technology,\n  or review mechanisms; and concessions or landlord contributions in the               overlap and existing-premises exit or continuing-liability effects.\n  periods and under the conditions where they apply.\n                                                                                       For tenant improvements, distinguish gross project cost, landlord\n  Full-service, gross, modified-gross and net are labels describing how                allowance and the tenant's net funded amount. An allowance is not the\n  costs are allocated. For each proposal, identify included and excluded               same measure as project cost.\n  components rather than comparing the labels or opening rents alone.\n\n\n\nNORMALIZATION CONTROLS\n\n Control                       Required treatment                                                                       If missing or different\n\n Area basis                    Identify rentable, usable or another stated convention for each premises. Use the        Do not assume a load factor or convert between\n                               same convention for both cases.                                                          rentable and usable area without a known source\n                                                                                                                        and conversion basis.\n\n Case scope                    Renewal includes both existing offices and their combined economic effect.               An omitted premises or liability makes the case\n                               Consolidation includes the headquarters plus exit, overlap, sublease or                  incomplete, not cheaper.\n                               continuing-liability effects for existing offices, where applicable.\n\n Term and timing               Use a common evaluation horizon, or disclose and reconcile unlike terms. Record          Do not turn a time-bound concession into a\n                               commencement, possession and any simultaneous-occupancy period. Assign free              permanent rent reduction.\n                               rent to the periods where it applies.\n\n Price and escalation          Use a common valuation or pricing base date and include contractual rent steps,          Post-cutoff changes need their own date and\n                               indexation or review mechanisms over the horizon.                                        reconciliation; opening rent alone is insufficient.\n\n Financial basis               State currency, tax treatment and nominal-versus-real basis. Where cash timing           Unlabeled bases cannot be compared as if they\n                               materially differs, use a stated present-value convention; otherwise label               were equivalent.\n                               undiscounted totals and their limitation.\n\n Uncertainty and outputs       Use ranges, scenarios or sensitivity for uncertain recoveries, fit-out, timing or exit   Do not create point precision from an unresolved\n                               costs. Show whole-period cost and a clearly labeled annual or annualized view from       input.\n                               the same components.\n\n Per-area view                 Only show a per-area measure when both the area convention and the cost                  A square-foot number with a different denominator\n                               numerator are the same for both cases.                                                   or component scope is not like-for-like.\n\n\n\n  Minimum output convention\n\n  Present two visibly labeled layers for each case: (1) recurring occupancy-cost subtotal and (2) all-in decision cost, which adds the one-time\n  implementation and exit effects. Both must use the same horizon, base date and component definitions. A missing amount remains\n  unresolved; it is not silently omitted or entered as zero.\n\n\n\n\nClient-facing working assessment | No commercial commitment                                                                                      14 March 2029 | Page 3 of 6\n\fMARCHBANK COMPONENTS / SHARED-PROPERTY TEAM                                                                                            COMPARISON READINESS\n\n\n\n\n03 / NEUTRAL DECISION CONDITIONS\n\nWhat would have to be true\nThese are testable conditions, not a scorecard and not a recommendation. None is confirmed on the material supplied\nfor this initial assessment.\n\n  Renewal of both existing offices                                                  Consolidation into one headquarters\n  Could be economically or operationally preferable only if the following           Could be economically or operationally preferable only if the following\n  conditions are evidenced:                                                         conditions are evidenced:\n\n   - The combined recurring and all-in decision cost of both existing               - Verified recurring and all-in economics for the one-headquarters case\n      offices is verified over a common horizon and base date, with                    show any claimed benefit only after fit-out, move, technology,\n      recoveries, utilities/services, escalations, concessions, fit-out and            overlap, transaction and existing-office exit or continuing-liability\n      material ancillary costs included where applicable.                              costs are included on the same basis.\n\n   - Any renewal work, transaction cost, local move, overlap or restoration         - Headquarters availability, area convention, term, recoveries,\n      obligation is identified; no missing value is treated as zero or left            escalations, concessions and landlord allowance are confirmed or\n      outside the option total.                                                        clearly labeled; asking, indicative and expected negotiated terms are\n                                                                                       not treated as commitments.\n   - Both locations have verified capacity and functional fit for Ironvale's\n      needs, with acceptable access, continuity and timing; operational             - Commencement, possession and the move plan are feasible, and\n      disruption is assessed separately from cost.                                     termination, surrender, make-good/restoration, sublease or\n                                                                                       continuing obligations for both existing offices are resolved or\n   - Sources, effective dates, assumptions and sensitivities are visible,              transparently carried in the model.\n      and the less favorable office has not been omitted from the case.\n                                                                                    - The headquarters has verified capacity and functional fit, with an\n                                                                                       acceptable transition and level of operational disruption; unresolved\n                                                                                       variables are shown through ranges or scenarios.\n\n\n\nSHARED DECISION LENS\n\n FINANCIAL                                               OPERATIONAL                                           GOVERNANCE\n\n Use normalized recurring and all-in components,         Keep capacity, functional fit, possession timing      Ironvale Freight makes the option decision. The\n not headline rent alone. Any conclusion must            and operational disruption visible as separate        designated pack approver controls changes to\n survive material uncertainty in recoveries, fit-out,    considerations. Do not monetize unsupported           inputs and labels before release; the broker\n timing and exit costs.                                  effects.                                              reports readiness and limitations.\n\n\n\n   NEUTRAL RELEASE LANGUAGE\n\n   No alternative is selected by this assessment\n   The pack may describe the conditions that would support either case. It should not preserve a previous consolidation conclusion merely for consistency\n   with earlier communication, and it should not replace that conclusion with a renewal conclusion without evidence.\n\n\n\n\nClient-facing working assessment | No commercial commitment                                                                             14 March 2029 | Page 4 of 6\n\fMARCHBANK COMPONENTS / SHARED-PROPERTY TEAM                                                                                             COMPARISON READINESS\n\n\n\n\n04 / PRESENTATION CHANGEOVER\n\nOne pack that remains clear after the cutover\nIronvale may continue using the pack after the end-state standard takes effect. The artifact therefore needs a visible\nbridge between the permitted legacy view and the controlling normalized view.\n\nTRANSITION RULE\n\n View                                       Permitted role                                       Required treatment\n\n Legacy headline rent per square foot       May appear through 31 March 2029 for continuity or   Label visibly: Legacy continuity metric - incomplete economic\n                                            context.                                             view; not a decision measure. Do not use it to rank the alternatives.\n\n Normalized occupancy-cost view             Required from 1 April 2029 and the controlling       Show recurring subtotal and all-in decision cost with component\n                                            economic view after cutover.                         schedule, dates, status labels and unresolved items. It is the\n                                                                                                 controlling view, not a co-equal second answer.\n\n\n\nTRACEABLE BRIDGE\n\n   1     Legacy context                     Retain the historical headline rate only with its source, as-of date, lease-structure label and area convention.\n\n\n   2     Recurring schedule                 Map base rent, applicable recoveries/pass-throughs, utilities/services, escalations and time-bound concessions into\n                                            the recurring subtotal for each case.\n\n\n   3     Implementation schedule            Show gross fit-out cost, landlord allowance and tenant net funded amount, then\n                                            move/furniture/equipment/technology, transaction, overlap and exit/carrying effects.\n\n\n   4     Decision view                      Use the same component numerator and area denominator for whole-period, annualized and any per-area output.\n                                            Explain every difference between the legacy and normalized views.\n\n\n\n  Why headline rent can mislead\n\n  A headline rent per square foot may bundle different charges under full-service, gross, modified-gross or net structures. Recoveries can sit\n  outside the headline; free rent and landlord contributions can be time-bound; fit-out and move costs can be absent; and rentable and usable\n  areas can use different denominators. The metric is therefore useful for continuity only when its limits are visible, not as proof that one\n  alternative costs less.\n\n\n\n  Version control rule\n\n  Keep the frozen 28 February 2029 snapshot as the baseline. Any information received after that cutoff is separately dated, labeled and\n  reconciled; it does not silently overwrite the baseline. Reconcile the client-email conflict before release. After 1 April 2029, the normalized\n  view controls even if the legacy view remains visible as historical context.\n\n\n\n\nClient-facing working assessment | No commercial commitment                                                                               14 March 2029 | Page 5 of 6\n\fMARCHBANK COMPONENTS / SHARED-PROPERTY TEAM                                                                                                         COMPARISON READINESS\n\n\n\n\n05 / ACTION AND RELEASE GATE\n\nCorrect the pack before the decision\nThe sequence below protects the 22 March 2029 decision without selecting an alternative. Target dates are working\ncompletion points for the correction effort; they are not market or lease facts.\n\nPRIORITIZED CORRECTIVE ACTIONS\n\n Priority     Responsible role                      Expected output                                                                            Target completion\n\n P1           Designated pack owner with            Withdraw or qualify the circulating lowest-cost label; issue neutral client wording that   Before any corrected pack is\n              Antoni                                classifies it as prior communication pending normalized evidence.                          circulated; 14 March handoff\n\n P1           Lease evidence owner / broker         Dated source ledger and abstracts for both existing offices plus the headquarters          15-17 March\n                                                    proposal or availability record: term, area, included/excluded charges, escalations,\n                                                    concessions and exit provisions.\n\n P1           Finance or model owner                Common-basis comparison schedule with recurring subtotal and all-in decision cost,         18-19 March\n                                                    whole-period and annualized views, area convention, currency/tax/nominal-real\n                                                    basis, timing treatment and ranges for uncertain inputs.\n\n P2           Operations / facilities owner         Capacity and functional-fit check, possession and move timing, overlap, disruption,        19 March\n                                                    and material parking, storage or signage requirements for both cases.\n\n P2           Antoni / comparison reviewer          Reconciled snapshot-versus-email change log, evidence labels,                              20 March\n                                                    gap-to-consequence register and legacy-to-normalized bridge reviewed in parallel\n                                                    for both cases.\n\n P3           Designated pack approver              After P1/P2 evidence review, approve changed inputs and labels; apply final                21 March, before decision\n                                                    presentation only when unresolved items are visible and no unsupported option\n                                                    label remains.\n\n\n\nRELEASE GATE FOR OONA\n\n  As at 14 March 2029, release this document as a readiness assessment only. A choice-supporting comparison can be issued\n  before 22 March only when each material input has a source and effective/as-of date; both cases use the same component scope,\n  horizon, base date and financial basis; the snapshot conflict is reconciled; the prior lowest-cost statement is withdrawn or clearly\n  qualified; and the designated approver records every remaining unresolved item. If those conditions are not met, say so plainly\n  and do not state that either alternative is lowest cost.\n\n\n\nCLIENT DISCLOSURES AND LIMITATIONS\n\n Reader needs to know                    Disclosure\n\n No source pack reviewed                 The frozen market snapshot, client emails, lease abstracts and financial model were not supplied for this initial assessment. No market,\n                                         lease, area, availability or cost figure is verified here.\n\n Commercial status                       Asking terms, indicative quotations and expected negotiated terms are different statuses and require verification. Availability is\n                                         time-sensitive and must be revalidated close to the decision. Final obligations arise from reviewed and executed documents, not from\n                                         this assessment.\n\n Scope and authority                     This is an evidence and communication-readiness assessment under the stated presentation changeover. It is not a jurisdiction-specific\n                                         legal, tax or accounting opinion, does not create an offer or commitment, and does not select an office strategy.\n\n Missing information                     Unknown monetary, area and timing inputs remain unresolved; they are not zero and are not silently omitted. Assumptions and\n                                         estimates must be labeled and tested with ranges or scenarios.\n\n\nPrepared for a broad public audience. Correspondence is summarized only; no personal contact details or unnecessary quotations are reproduced.\n\n\n\n\nClient-facing working assessment | No commercial commitment                                                                                           14 March 2029 | Page 6 of 6\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R001","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required file exists as an openable PDF with the exact filename `ironvale_lease_comparison_compliance_assessment.pdf`.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The submitted top-level file and its PDF structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename, that the file is a genuine PDF, and that all pages render without an error."},{"rubric_item_id":"R002","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete submission contains no more than six rendered pages, counting any cover, notes or appendix.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count of the submitted PDF.","grader_hint":"Count every rendered page in the PDF; blank and appendix pages count toward the limit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R003","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment identifies Ironvale Freight’s 22 March 2029 decision and accurately names the alternatives as renewal of two existing offices versus consolidation into one headquarters.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening scope or purpose language in the PDF.","grader_hint":"Look for the organization, decision date and both alternatives; equivalent wording for the alternatives is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R004","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment states that 28 February is the frozen market-evidence cutoff and distinguishes that date from the later decision date.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope, evidence-status or limitations language.","grader_hint":"Confirm that 28 February is treated as the snapshot date rather than the decision or report date."},{"rubric_item_id":"R005","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The assessment gives an unambiguous present-status finding that the available material does not substantiate consolidation as the lowest-cost option and that a normalized comparison is not yet decision-ready.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall assessment finding and any readiness label.","grader_hint":"Accept plain-language equivalents such as “not demonstrated,” “not supportable on the current record” or “pending comparable evidence.” The finding must not be buried or contradicted elsewhere."},{"rubric_item_id":"R006","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document frames its function as an assessment of evidence, comparison readiness and client communication, while leaving the option decision with Ironvale Freight.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Purpose, scope and decision-authority statements.","grader_hint":"Check that the broker’s assessment role and the client’s decision role are both intelligible to a non-specialist."},{"rubric_item_id":"R007","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"“Occupancy cost” is defined in plain language before its first substantive use as a comparison result or decision condition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The first occurrences and definition of “occupancy cost.”","grader_hint":"Find the first substantive use and verify that a definition appears before or at that point; a later glossary alone is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R008","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The defined recurring-cost component includes fixed or base rent.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Occupancy-cost definition or comparison requirements.","grader_hint":"Accept base rent, fixed rent or minimum rent as equivalent concepts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R009","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The defined recurring-cost component includes applicable operating-expense recoveries or pass-throughs, such as common-area charges, property taxes or insurance.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Occupancy-cost definition or comparison requirements.","grader_hint":"The document need not claim every charge applies, but it must require applicable recoveries to be identified rather than hidden inside a lease label."},{"rubric_item_id":"R010","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment accounts for separately paid utilities and occupancy services where they are not included in rent.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cost-component requirements.","grader_hint":"Look for utilities and at least a general treatment of separately contracted building or occupancy services."},{"rubric_item_id":"R011","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Concessions and landlord contributions are treated as economic offsets with timing and conditions, rather than as stand-alone promotional labels.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment of free rent, allowances or other concessions.","grader_hint":"Credit requires more than naming concessions; the text must indicate that their value depends on timing, eligibility or conditions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R012","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The definition or required comparison scope includes tenant-funded fit-out and transaction or transition costs needed to occupy the premises.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Occupancy-cost definition and comparison scope.","grader_hint":"Confirm that one-time costs are not excluded merely because they are not rent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R013","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document resolves the two common meanings of “occupancy cost” by keeping recurring occupancy charges and one-time implementation costs visibly distinguishable while specifying how both enter the decision comparison.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions, labels and relationship between recurring and total decision cost.","grader_hint":"A defensible solution may use recurring and all-in subtotals or similarly explicit labels. Reject a single undefined total that leaves readers unable to tell whether one-time costs are included."},{"rubric_item_id":"R014","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Full-service, gross, modified-gross and net lease descriptions are treated as allocation labels rather than proof that two headline rents are directly comparable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Lease-structure discussion or comparison requirements.","grader_hint":"The assessment should explain that the included and excluded cost components must be identified for each proposal, regardless of label."},{"rubric_item_id":"R015","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison requires the area basis to be identified as rentable, usable or another stated convention for each premises.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Area-definition requirements.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit area terminology rather than an unexplained square-foot figure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R016","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment prohibits or qualifies conversion between area conventions unless the source and conversion basis are known.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Area-normalization discussion.","grader_hint":"Credit language warning against assumed load factors or unsupported rentable-to-usable conversions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R017","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The renewal alternative is defined to include both existing offices, with their combined economic effect retained rather than presenting only the more favorable site.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternative scope and comparison requirements.","grader_hint":"Verify that both renewal premises belong to the same option case and that omission of either would be treated as incomplete."},{"rubric_item_id":"R018","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The consolidation alternative includes both the new-headquarters occupancy economics and the consequences of exiting or carrying the existing offices.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternative scope and consolidation-cost requirements.","grader_hint":"A new-site rent alone is insufficient; look for exit, overlap, termination, sublease or continuing-liability effects as applicable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R019","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Both alternatives are required to use the same evaluation horizon or to disclose and reconcile any unavoidable difference in term.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Time-horizon requirements.","grader_hint":"Check for a common comparison period or an explicit adjustment for unlike lease terms."},{"rubric_item_id":"R020","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Both alternatives are required to use a common valuation or pricing base date.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Normalization requirements.","grader_hint":"The document should prevent costs captured at different dates from being compared without adjustment or disclosure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R021","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Commencement dates, possession timing and any period of simultaneous occupancy are identified as comparison inputs.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing and transition-cost requirements.","grader_hint":"Look for timing dependencies that could create overlap, delay or unusable space."},{"rubric_item_id":"R022","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Contractual rent escalations or review mechanisms are included over the evaluation period rather than comparing only opening rent.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rent-normalization requirements.","grader_hint":"Accept references to fixed steps, indexation or review assumptions, provided the full period is addressed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R023","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Free-rent periods are assigned to the periods in which they apply and are not treated as a permanent reduction in rent.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concession-treatment requirements.","grader_hint":"Check that timing is part of the treatment, even though no numerical schedule is available."},{"rubric_item_id":"R024","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Tenant-improvement economics distinguish gross project cost, landlord allowance and the tenant’s net funded amount.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Fit-out comparison requirements.","grader_hint":"The relationship should make clear that an allowance cannot be compared with project cost as though they were the same measure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R025","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Moving, furniture, equipment, technology and business-relocation costs are recognized as potentially different between renewal and consolidation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition-cost requirements.","grader_hint":"A grouped category is acceptable if it clearly encompasses physical move and workplace-enablement costs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R026","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Termination, surrender, make-good or restoration obligations for existing premises are included where applicable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Existing-lease exit requirements.","grader_hint":"The assessment may state that applicability is unverified; it must still identify the category as decision-relevant."},{"rubric_item_id":"R027","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Material ancillary occupancy costs such as parking, storage or signage are required to be treated consistently between alternatives.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ancillary-cost requirements.","grader_hint":"Credit a representative, clearly non-exhaustive category rather than requiring every example word."},{"rubric_item_id":"R028","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison standard calls for both a whole-period cost and a clearly labeled annual or annualized view, with each derived from the same included components.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Required output measures and their definitions.","grader_hint":"The two views must not silently use different component scopes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R029","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any per-area measure is conditional on use of the same area convention and cost numerator for both alternatives.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Unit-cost and area-normalization language.","grader_hint":"Look for both denominator comparability and numerator comparability; a generic warning about square feet alone is incomplete."},{"rubric_item_id":"R030","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment requires time-value treatment for materially different cash-flow timing or an explicit explanation when undiscounted totals are used.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Financial-normalization requirements.","grader_hint":"Accept present-value treatment, a stated discount convention, or a clear limitation explaining why only undiscounted totals are shown."},{"rubric_item_id":"R031","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Uncertain recoveries, fit-out costs, timing or exit costs are assigned ranges, scenarios or sensitivity treatment rather than false point precision.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Uncertainty and sensitivity requirements.","grader_hint":"At least one defensible uncertainty treatment must be specified for material variable inputs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R032","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Currency, tax treatment and nominal-versus-real basis are required to be stated where they affect comparison totals.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Financial-basis requirements.","grader_hint":"The document may mark these as unresolved; it must prevent an unlabeled mixture of bases."},{"rubric_item_id":"R033","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Missing monetary, area or timing inputs are not treated as zero and are not silently omitted from option totals.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Missing-data conventions and limitations.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit missing, pending or not-applicable distinction and a warning against defaulting unknowns to zero."},{"rubric_item_id":"R034","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The frozen 28 February snapshot is identified as the required baseline for market evidence while being clearly labeled as not supplied or not reviewed for this assessment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-scope and source-status statements.","grader_hint":"The document must not imply that the author inspected the snapshot; it should preserve its baseline role and disclose the review limitation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R035","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment records that client-email descriptions conflict with the frozen snapshot without inventing the disputed terms or deciding which source is factually correct.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conflict description and evidence limitations.","grader_hint":"Credit neutral identification of the conflict. Reject fabricated details about rent, area, availability or concessions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R036","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The earlier statement that consolidation would be presented as the lowest-cost option is explicitly identified as a claim requiring substantiation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claim-status discussion.","grader_hint":"The individual’s name need not be repeated, but the substance of the circulating claim must be recognizable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R037","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The earlier lowest-cost statement is classified as prior communication rather than converted into a comparison input, verified fact or presentation label.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Relationship between the prior statement and the evidence model.","grader_hint":"Confirm that repetition or seniority is not treated as evidence and that the claim does not control the assessment result."},{"rubric_item_id":"R038","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The proposed comparison requires each material input to carry an identifiable source and effective or as-of date.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-label requirements.","grader_hint":"A general statement applying to all material inputs is sufficient; no populated source table is required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R039","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Evidence labels distinguish verified fact, third-party statement or quote, estimate, assumption and unresolved item.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-status vocabulary and definitions.","grader_hint":"Equivalent plain-language labels are acceptable if a reader can distinguish these different confidence states."},{"rubric_item_id":"R040","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Asking terms, indicative quotations and expected negotiated terms are not presented as interchangeable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Commercial-term status guidance.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit distinction between marketed or indicative terms and committed or negotiated economics."},{"rubric_item_id":"R041","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Availability is treated as time-sensitive and subject to confirmation rather than as a guaranteed condition.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Availability status and limitations.","grader_hint":"The assessment should require revalidation close to the decision or identify availability as provisional."},{"rubric_item_id":"R042","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Information received after the frozen cutoff is required to be separately dated, labeled and reconciled rather than silently overwriting the snapshot.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Post-cutoff change-control requirements.","grader_hint":"Credit any clear version-control convention that preserves the cutoff baseline and makes later changes visible."},{"rubric_item_id":"R043","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each material evidence gap is linked to the comparison component or decision claim it prevents the reader from validating.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Gap register, corrective-action content or readiness discussion.","grader_hint":"A bare list of missing documents is insufficient; the decision consequence of each material gap must be intelligible."},{"rubric_item_id":"R044","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment establishes a controlled owner or approval point for changing comparison inputs and labels before release.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Governance or corrective-action requirements.","grader_hint":"The exact job title may be locally chosen, but responsibility for accepting changes and releasing the corrected comparison must not be diffuse."},{"rubric_item_id":"R045","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition rule is accurately stated: the legacy view may still appear through 31 March, while the normalized occupancy-cost view is required from 1 April.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition-period explanation.","grader_hint":"Confirm both dates and their different effects; do not credit wording that treats the legacy view as mandatory."},{"rubric_item_id":"R046","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"During the transition, any headline rent-per-square-foot view is limited to contextual or continuity use and is visibly labeled as not a complete economic comparison.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legacy-view controls and labels.","grader_hint":"The legacy metric may be shown, but the document must prevent it from being interpreted as the option ranking."},{"rubric_item_id":"R047","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The assessment gives a clear non-specialist warning that headline rent per square foot can mislead when lease structures, recoveries, concessions, fit-out economics or area definitions differ.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reader warning or comparison explanation.","grader_hint":"The warning should explain why the metric misleads, not merely say that it is discouraged."},{"rubric_item_id":"R048","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition treatment provides a traceable bridge between legacy figures and normalized components, so a reader can tell why the two views may differ.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Relationship between legacy and normalized presentation.","grader_hint":"A mapping, reconciliation principle or component explanation is acceptable; duplicate unexplained totals are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R049","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The end-state standard makes the normalized, component-complete comparison the controlling economic view after the cutover.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-state requirements.","grader_hint":"The document must not leave readers with two co-equal methods after 1 April."},{"rubric_item_id":"R050","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Defined terms and status labels are used consistently throughout the assessment, including in findings, decision conditions and corrective actions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Terminology across the entire PDF.","grader_hint":"Check especially that “occupancy cost,” “verified,” “estimate” and “availability” do not change meaning between sections."},{"rubric_item_id":"R051","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The two alternatives receive neutral, parallel treatment with comparable scope, evidentiary labels and level of detail.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternative descriptions, conditions and gap treatment.","grader_hint":"Look for framing bias, asymmetric caveats or more favorable labels for one option even when the document avoids an express recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R052","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment states concrete conditions that could make renewal of both offices economically or operationally preferable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Renewal decision conditions.","grader_hint":"Conditions should be testable, such as verified renewal economics, avoided transition costs, sufficient capacity or acceptable operational continuity, not generic advantages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R053","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The assessment states concrete conditions that could make consolidation economically or operationally preferable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Consolidation decision conditions.","grader_hint":"Conditions should be testable, such as sufficient verified savings after transition costs, fit-for-purpose capacity and feasible move timing."},{"rubric_item_id":"R054","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Financial decision conditions are tied to normalized cost components and unresolved variables rather than to headline rent alone.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Financial conditions for both alternatives.","grader_hint":"A condition such as “consolidation is cheaper only if verified recurring and one-time savings exceed exit and move costs” is acceptable without a fabricated threshold."},{"rubric_item_id":"R055","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Material non-cost considerations are identified separately from the financial comparison, including capacity or functional fit, timing and operational disruption.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational decision conditions.","grader_hint":"The document should not monetize unsupported operational effects or blend them invisibly into a cost label."},{"rubric_item_id":"R056","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Confidence and uncertainty language is proportionate to the absent evidence and does not create false precision through unsupported scoring, percentages or categorical certainty.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Findings, status labels and decision-condition language.","grader_hint":"Assess whether a public reader would understand what is known, provisional and unknown without being given a manufactured sense of accuracy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R057","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Corrective actions identify a responsible role, a specific evidence or review output and a completion point for each material workstream.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Corrective-action or next-step content.","grader_hint":"Names are not required, but vague actions such as “review leases” without an owner and output are insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R058","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Corrective actions are prioritized in a sequence that protects the 22 March decision, with claim correction and decision-critical evidence addressed before presentation polish.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Action priority and timing.","grader_hint":"Check that critical comparability and substantiation work precedes cosmetic or nonessential refinement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R059","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The assessment provides a client-appropriate way to correct the circulating lowest-cost statement without blaming an individual or implying that the ultimate option decision has already been made.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Corrective communication or release condition.","grader_hint":"Look for neutral language such as reclassifying the statement as provisional or withdrawing the label pending normalized evidence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R060","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Commercial limitations explain that availability and proposed terms require verification and that final obligations arise from reviewed and executed documents, not from the assessment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations or disclosure language.","grader_hint":"The disclosure should be specific enough to distinguish the assessment from an offer, commitment or lease document."},{"rubric_item_id":"R061","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document minimizes sensitive correspondence by describing the conflict and prior statement without reproducing personal contact details or unnecessary email quotations.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All references to people and correspondence.","grader_hint":"Names needed for accountability are permissible; penalizing disclosure is not warranted unless contact details or unnecessary message content is reproduced."},{"rubric_item_id":"R062","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A broad non-specialist reader can understand the assessment’s finding, key definitions, unresolved risks and required corrections without needing to ask the author how to interpret a lease term or status label.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The complete rendered PDF.","grader_hint":"Judge plain language, defined jargon, navigability and whether the document functions as a standalone client-facing assessment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R063","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Findings, labels and corrective actions agree with one another; no item described as unverified elsewhere is presented as settled in a decision condition or summary.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-check of summary, body, labels and actions within the PDF.","grader_hint":"Trace the main claims—lowest cost, availability, lease terms and comparison readiness—through the document and flag any internal contradiction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R064","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The assessment is professionally usable at the stated deadline: it is concise, visibly distinguishes findings from conditions and actions, and gives Oona Hollingsworth a practical basis for deciding whether the client pack can be released.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The complete rendered PDF.","grader_hint":"Evaluate the artifact as a real release-readiness document, not merely as a list of real-estate concepts."},{"rubric_item_id":"P001","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The submission invents or presents unsupported market rents, lease terms, areas, cost amounts, discount rates, savings, rankings or other quantitative facts.","weight":-8.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All figures, tables, examples and claims in the PDF.","grader_hint":"Apply once if any externally factual quantitative value is fabricated. Do not penalize dates and transition rules supplied in the request or clearly non-factual structural labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"P002","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The submission selects, scores, ranks or endorses an alternative, or repeats consolidation as the lowest-cost option as though substantiated.","weight":-10.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive finding, option labels, scores, recommendation language and closing statements.","grader_hint":"Apply once for an express or functionally equivalent recommendation. Neutral conditional statements about what would make an option preferable are not a violation."},{"rubric_item_id":"P003","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The submission asserts jurisdiction-specific legal compliance, guarantees lease availability or terms, or certifies that the assessment itself creates a binding commercial commitment.","weight":-7.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal, regulatory and commercial-certainty statements throughout the PDF.","grader_hint":"Apply once when the document overclaims authority or certainty. A limitation directing legal or tax questions to qualified advisers is not penalized."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Marchbank Components’ shared-property team is supporting affiliate Ironvale Freight through an enterprise office decision while its lease-comparison practice is changing. The broker may assess evidence and communication readiness but has no authority to choose an option for the client.","difficulty_sources":["Reconciling an unsupported prior assurance with a neutral evidence-readiness assessment","Defining a like-for-like economic comparison without access to lease or market figures","Serving both a temporary legacy presentation practice and its normalized end state","Resolving an ambiguous cost term for a broad non-specialist audience","Separating factual status, assumptions, decision conditions and recommendations under a strict page limit"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_material":{"files":[],"constraint":"No source pack is created. The solver must rely on the request-stated facts and established commercial-leasing comparison practice."},"intended_verdict":"The current communication is not ready to support a lowest-cost label because the source conflict has not been reconciled and the alternatives have not been normalized. This is a readiness finding, not a recommendation between options.","required_reasoning_relationships":["The frozen snapshot remains the baseline as of its stated cutoff, but because it was not supplied or reviewed, its contents cannot be asserted. Later statements must be separately dated and reconciled rather than silently substituted.","A prior internal or client-facing statement is communication history, not evidence. It cannot determine an option label or comparison result.","A defensible occupancy-cost convention retains a recurring-cost subtotal while also incorporating one-time fit-out, transaction, move, overlap and exit effects into the full decision comparison. This resolves the two audience interpretations without hiding either.","Headline rent per area is not comparable across different lease structures unless included recoveries, concessions and area conventions are normalized. It may remain visible during the transition only as a clearly subordinate continuity measure.","The renewal case contains both existing premises. The consolidation case contains the replacement headquarters plus the economic consequences of leaving or carrying the existing premises.","Like-for-like comparison requires a common evaluation horizon, base date, area convention, component scope and treatment of timing. Any unavoidable mismatch must be disclosed and reconciled.","Missing values remain missing and decision-relevant; they do not become zero. Each material gap is connected to the claim or calculation it prevents.","Decision conditions are symmetric and conditional. They identify what evidence would make either alternative economically or operationally preferable without assigning a score or making a choice.","Corrective work is ordered by decision impact: withdraw or qualify unsupported labels, secure decision-critical lease and market evidence, normalize the alternatives, review the corrected claims, and only then finalize presentation.","The transition-period artifact must remain intelligible after the request-stated cutover. The normalized comparison controls in the end state, while any legacy view remains explicitly non-controlling."],"imperfections_to_preserve":["A conflict exists between client correspondence and a frozen market snapshot, but the disputed details are intentionally unavailable.","An earlier lowest-cost assurance is already circulating and creates pressure to preserve an unsupported conclusion.","The comparison practice is mid-transition, so legacy and end-state presentations must coexist temporarily without appearing co-equal.","The central cost term has two established audience interpretations and must be resolved through explicit component scope.","No numerical lease, area, market or cost data are available; any fabricated figure must fail the over-production controls."],"gold_content_constraints":["Produce a client-facing PDF within the request-stated page cap.","State the present lack of substantiation prominently and consistently.","Define all evidence-status labels and the occupancy-cost convention before relying on them.","Cover recurring lease charges, recoveries, concessions, fit-out, move, overlap, exit and material ancillary costs without claiming that every category applies to every lease.","Treat area definitions, lease structures, term, timing and valuation basis as normalization controls.","Include balanced decision conditions and prioritized corrective actions with roles and expected outputs.","Use no invented figures, rankings, jurisdiction-specific legal claims or recommendation.","Minimize correspondence details and avoid personal contact information."],"offline_validation":"An imagined reference PDF was walked against all rubric predicates using only the request facts and occupational knowledge. Every credit item can be satisfied without external research or invented figures, and all three penalty behaviors can be avoided. The artifact can be authored and rendered offline with standard document-generation libraries."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"freeform"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"compliance_assessment","filename":"ironvale_lease_comparison_compliance_assessment.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"Client-facing compliance and evidence-readiness assessment for Ironvale Freight’s office lease alternatives.","required":true,"constraints":["Place the file at the top level of the working directory.","Use the exact required filename.","Submit one openable PDF containing no more than six pages, including any cover, notes or appendix.","Write for a broad non-specialist audience.","Do not invent market, lease, area or cost figures.","Present facts and decision conditions without selecting, ranking or endorsing an alternative."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"compliance_assessment","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"real_estate_brokers","sector":"real_estate_rental_leasing","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution must be reviewed independently by one experienced commercial tenant-representation broker and one plain-language or client-communications compliance reviewer. Reviewers must verify that every claim is supportable from the request and occupational practice, that no jurisdiction-specific legal conclusion or unsupported figure is introduced, and that the six-page PDF remains usable for a non-specialist audience.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No lease abstracts, market snapshot, client emails or financial model are supplied, so the document can assess readiness and define compliant comparison requirements but cannot calculate a preferred option.","The assessment is not a jurisdiction-specific legal, tax or accounting opinion.","The reference solution cannot validate actual market availability or commercial terms and must label those matters as pending evidence."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T04:57:08+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T04:57:08+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T04:57:08+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T04:57:08+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/f391eb60ff6f0e0b","declared_deliverables":["ironvale_lease_comparison_compliance_assessment.pdf"]};
